<h2>The definitions that open the door</h2>
<p>Three definitions in Section 2 set the Act's true breadth:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Definition</th><th>Text in substance</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Aggrieved person — s. 2(a)</td><td>Any woman who is, or has been, in a domestic relationship with the respondent and alleges domestic violence</td><td>No requirement of marriage; daughters, sisters, mothers and widows qualify</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Domestic relationship — s. 2(f)</td><td>Two persons who live or have at any point lived together in a shared household, related by consanguinity, marriage, a relationship in the nature of marriage, adoption, or as members of a joint family</td><td>Blood relationships and joint-family living are enough</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shared household — s. 2(s)</td><td>The household where the aggrieved person lives or has lived in a domestic relationship, whether or not she has any right, title or interest in it</td><td>The parental or joint-family home counts, even if she owns no share</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Read together, these provisions mean the Act follows the <em>relationship and the roof</em>, not the marital tie. The violence it addresses — physical, sexual, verbal and emotional, and economic abuse under Section 3 — occurs in parental homes too, and Parliament's text does not look away from it.</p>

<h2>Situations the Act reaches</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>The daughter at home</h4><p>An adult or minor daughter facing beatings, confinement, or threats over her choices — education, employment, marriage — may seek protection orders, and economic abuse (withholding her stridhan, her salary, her documents) is actionable in itself.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The sister and the family house</h4><p>Brothers locking a sister out of the family home after the parents' death, or coercing her to relinquish her share, confront Section 17: her right to reside in the shared household does not depend on ownership, and dispossession without procedure established by law can be restrained.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The widowed or deserted daughter who returns</h4><p>A woman back in her parental home after widowhood or separation re-enters a domestic relationship by consanguinity. Harassment there is within the Act, and monetary relief can address her maintenance and medical needs.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The mother against her son</h4><p>An elderly mother facing abuse or eviction by a son may proceed under the Act — alongside the distinct machinery of the senior citizens legislation — and claim residence and monetary protection.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What must be pleaded and proved</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The domestic relationship:</strong> the blood, marriage, adoption or joint-family tie, and the fact of living together in a shared household at some point — ration cards, Aadhaar addresses, school records and photographs commonly prove it.</li>
    <li><strong>The domestic violence:</strong> acts falling within Section 3, pleaded with dates and particulars; economic abuse — deprivation of resources the woman is entitled to by the relationship, or disposal of household assets — deserves specific attention in natal-family cases.</li>
    <li><strong>The relief sought:</strong> each of Sections 18 to 22 has its own ingredients; residence relief should identify the shared household precisely, and monetary relief should be quantified.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The limits to keep in view</h2>
<p>The Act is a protective statute, not a partition suit. The Magistrate under the Act cannot adjudicate title to the family property or divide it among heirs; questions of ownership and succession belong to the civil court. The residence order protects possession and occupation, not inheritance. Equally, the domestic relationship must be real: a relative who never shared a household with the woman stands outside the definition, and proceedings against distant kin added indiscriminately invite quashing. Finally, the Act supplements rather than displaces other remedies — a criminal complaint for offences under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, a civil suit for injunction or partition, and senior-citizen proceedings can all run on their own tracks.</p>

<h2>Procedure in Delhi, in brief</h2>
<p>The application under Section 12 is filed before the Judicial Magistrate (in Delhi, the designated Mahila Courts) within whose jurisdiction the aggrieved woman resides, the respondent resides, or the cause of action arose. A Protection Officer can assist with the domestic incident report; interim and ex parte orders under Section 23 are available on the application itself; and breach of a protection order is a cognizable offence under Section 31. Appeals lie to the Court of Session under Section 29 within thirty days.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article explains the statutory position in general terms for information. Whether a particular family situation satisfies the definitions is a fact-specific question; this is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
